    "body": "## Overview\n\nCache the `kv_cache.transpose(1, 2).split(head_size, dim=-1)` result in `FlashAttentionImpl` keyed on `data_ptr()`, avoiding redundant view operations on every `forward()` and `do_kv_cache_update()` call.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Eliminates redundant `transpose(1, 2).split(head_size, dim=-1)` calls in the FlashAttention hot path\n- Applies to both `FlashAttentionImpl` and `FlashAttentionDiffKVImpl`\n- Clears cached views during profiling cleanup to avoid stale references\n\n## Validation\n\n- GPU correctness test on RTX 5090 (CUDA 13.0): verified cache hit returns same views, rebind triggers recompute, cached views match fresh computation, writes to kv_cache reflected in cached views\n- `ruff check` and `ruff format --check`: all passed\n- Existing `test_kv_head_stride_canonicalization.py` tests: 10 CPU tests passed\n\n## Details\n\nThe KV cache tensor is bound once via `bind_kv_cache()` and never reallocated during serving, making its `data_ptr()` stable. This allows caching the transpose+split views and reusing them across calls.\n\n`_get_kv_cache_views()` checks `data_ptr()` on each call. On the first call (or after a rebind with a new tensor), it computes and stores the views. Subsequent calls return the cached views directly.\n\n`FlashAttentionDiffKVImpl` inherits this method. Its `forward()` uses the same `split(self.head_size)` pattern, so the cached views work correctly. Its `do_kv_cache_update()` uses only `transpose(1, 2)` without split (passes to `triton_reshape_and_cache_flash_diffkv`), so it does not use the cached views.\n\nAlso added cleanup of `_kv_cache_view_cache` in `_cleanup_profiling_kv_cache()` alongside the existing `_k_scale_cache`/`_v_scale_cache` cleanup, to release old KV cache storage promptly after profiling.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
